{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head ee6ddcbcc52bb8ce8de376cc327fca56e62277f1, tree f5df209c49448c3734c8defbdda59b56ebf95380, against Main 2e228b3caa69fa08f9c56de3608c41e7408b8f9f. I read primary inventory d88c4724 first, examined all six exact-head Sources, and prepared this review under my own promise dc987722 with the original D1 implementation request 51bd88c6. Architecture, Security and Simplification: no blocking findings in this scope.\n\nScope and source review. This delivery closes finding2.5 only. The six authored paths are three additions and three modifications; all 2,728 other paths preserve Main's modes and blobs, for 2,734 candidate files. The five deployed Worker configurations and apps/room/fetch.ts composition are correctly distinguished from the scope package's default export. The inventory describes method/path families, weakest gate class, activation and preparation work before the final gate, state changes, bounds and outbound effects. It preserves public/self-signed creation and other known weaknesses rather than claiming to fix them. Creation/admission R1, other programme findings and deployment configuration remain outside this closure.\n\nThe source-driven matcher executes actual dispatcher declarations against labelled boundary stand-ins, compares method-route/gate bijections, and uses missing, extra and changed-gate controls. The real requests-to-request-window matcher mutation is meaningful with the inventory held fixed. Twenty-six owner seals constrain the remaining source context: twenty-five owner files are byte-identical to Main and the worker changes only two comments. Six previously recorded F2 owner seals were refreshed after source correspondence; the other twenty are unchanged. I reviewed that correspondence without executing candidate source or recomputing AST seals. These tests are structural evidence, not Native authorization, arbitrary request-body, resource-bound or deployed-settings proof.\n\nThe inventory corre